<title>BRAZIL: More rains seen for key Brazil soy area - Somar.</title>
<headline>More rains seen for key Brazil soy area - Somar.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's southern soybean growing regions should see more showers this week after a cold front brought up to 50 mm of rain at the end of last week, independent meteorologist Somar said.</p>
<p>A fresh cold front will be followed by a mass of polar cold air, causing a sharp drop in temperatures next weekend in the west of the region as well as in the state of Mato Grosso do Sul, Somar said in a weekly report.</p>
<p>Last week's rains brought some relief to parched soybean crops even though they came too late to reverse losses caused by weeks of drought in March and early April, Somar said.</p>
<p>&quot;However the rainfall prepare the ground for pasture and for the winter crops,&quot; it said.</p>
<p>Weekly average rainfall in key regions in milimetres was as follows:</p>
<p>    REGION		   TOTAL	  TOTAL	    AVERAGE</p>
<p>			    Apr 15-22    Apr 1-14	   April</p>
<p>Londrina (PR)	    26.0	   32.0	     100.0</p>
<p>Cascavel (PR)	    12.0	   14.0		95.0</p>
<p>Passo Fundo (RS)	 50.0	   54.0	     128.0</p>
<p>Sao Luis Gonzaga (RS)  32.0	   37.0	     177.0</p>
<p>Santa Maria (RS)	 45.0	   45.0	     141.0</p>
<p>Mato Grosso		12.0	   64.0	     115.0</p>
<p>((--Joelle Diderich, Sao Paulo newsrooom +55 11 248 5417))</p>
